"model": "<p><b>(a)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>N</th><th>Product</th><th>Times</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>6</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>After the third pass Times = 4 = N, so the loop stops. <b>Result: Product = 6</b> (the algorithm calculates (N − 1)! = 3! = 6).</p><p>(b) It calculates (N − 1)! — the product 1 × 2 × … × (N − 1). If N = 1, Times becomes 2 on the first pass and only increases, so <code>N = Times</code> is never true: an infinite loop. A pre-test loop or <code>Until Times &gt;= N</code> would fix it.</p><p>(c)</p><pre>START\n   REPEAT\n      PRINT \"Enter two different numbers\"\n      READ Num1, Num2\n      IF Num1 = Num2 THEN\n         PRINT \"Numbers must be different\"\n      ENDIF\n   UNTIL Num1 &lt;&gt; Num2\n   IF Num1 &lt; Num2 THEN\n      PRINT \"The smaller number is \", Num1\n   ELSE\n      PRINT \"The smaller number is \", Num2\n   ENDIF\nSTOP</pre><pre>program SmallerValidated;\nvar\n  Num1, Num2: integer;\nbegin\n  repeat\n    writeln('Enter two different numbers');\n    readln(Num1, Num2);\n    if Num1 = Num2 then\n      writeln('Numbers must be different');\n  until Num1 &lt;&gt; Num2;\n  if Num1 &lt; Num2 then\n    writeln('The smaller number is ', Num1)\n  else\n    writeln('The smaller number is ', Num2);\nend.</pre>",
"key": "<p><b>Mark scheme · Total 17 marks</b></p><ul><li>(a) 1 mark per correct row (3) and 1 for the result 6 (4). Follow-through for a consistent error once.</li><li>(b) 1 for stating that it calculates (N − 1)!, 2 for explaining why N = 1 gives an endless loop.</li><li>(c) Pseudocode 5 (loop, equality test, message, selection, output); Pascal 5 (declarations, loop, readln, if-else, writeln/syntax).</li></ul>",
"teacherTip": "In (a), REPEAT…UNTIL tests after the body, so there is no row with Times = 5 and the result is 6. In (b), 1 mark for (N − 1)! (or 'multiplies 1 × 2 × … × (N − 1)') and 2 for explaining that with N = 1, Times is already 2 at the first test and only grows, so the loop never ends. In (c), award 5 for the pseudocode (loop, equality test, message, selection, output) and 5 for the Pascal (declarations, loop, readln, if-else, writeln and syntax); the two numbers must be read again inside the loop until they are different."
