"model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Local area: the 30 computers in the Sandbox Tree College IT lab share one printer and a file server in the same building. Wide area: a bank connects its branches in Bridgetown, St. Lucia and St. Kitts so every teller can see customer accounts.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Coaxial cable: connecting a television in a home to the cable-TV provider's box — cheap and good enough over a short distance. Fibre-optic cable: the high-speed backbone linking two campus buildings or the undersea cable connecting the island to the Internet — long distance, very high bandwidth and no electrical interference.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Simplex: a radio station broadcasting to listeners (or the PJIA flight-information display), where data only needs to travel one way. Duplex: a telephone or WhatsApp call, where both people must talk and listen at the same time.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Bluetooth: pairing wireless earbuds or a speaker to a phone — short range, low power, no router needed. Wi-Fi: giving 200 hotel guests Internet access throughout the building — longer range, higher speed, many devices.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Satellite: a cruise ship at sea or a relief team after a hurricane, where there are no towers or cables. Microwave: a point-to-point link between a TelEm tower on a hill and a building in town that are in line of sight.</p><p>(f) Fibre would give the best speed but damaged cables and poles could take weeks to repair. A satellite terminal (e.g. a low-earth-orbit service) can be installed in a day and does not depend on local cables, though bandwidth is shared and heavy rain can affect it; latency is acceptable for browsing and video lessons. A microwave link from a working TelEm tower is also quick if there is line of sight. Inside, Wi-Fi access points are the obvious choice for 300 students' devices. Recommendation: a satellite (or microwave) link as the Internet connection, feeding several Wi-Fi access points, then move back to fibre when it is repaired.</p>",
"key": "<p><b>Mark scheme · Total 16 marks</b></p><p><b>Parts (a)–(e)</b></p><p>1 mark for EACH appropriate scenario (10). A definition without a scenario: 0. The scenario must show why that option is more useful than the other.</p><p><b>Part (f)</b></p><ul><li>(f) 2 per technology evaluated (4), 2 for justified recommendation.</li></ul>",
