"model": "<p><b>(a)</b> (i) 55. (ii) 4. (iii) 300. (iv) '...' means that some elements of the array are not shown — the sequence of values continues between the references displayed. (v) Integer.</p><p><b>(b)</b> 1 Declare the array; 2 Initialize the array; 3 Enter the number of payments for a student; 4 Output data for a student.</p><p><b>(c)</b> (i) It initialises the counter variable <code>count</code> to zero before counting starts, so the total begins at 0 (no leftover value). (ii) PAYMENT is the name of the one-dimensional array holding the number of payments (Num_of_payments) for each reference. (iii) The fragment loops through every element of the array from reference 1 to 300 and, each time a student's number of payments equals 5, adds 1 to <code>count</code>; so it counts how many students make five payments.</p><p>Complete test program (only the values shown are set; the rest default to 4):</p><pre>program Payments;\nvar\n  PAYMENT: array[1..300] of integer;\n  Reference, count: integer;\nbegin\n  for Reference := 1 to 300 do\n    PAYMENT[Reference] := 4;\n  PAYMENT[2] := 5; PAYMENT[55] := 3;\n  count := 0;\n  for Reference := 1 to 300 do\n    if (PAYMENT[Reference] = 5) then\n      count := count + 1;\n  writeln('Students making five payments: ', count);\nend.</pre><p>Python equivalent:</p><pre>payment = [4] * 300\npayment[1] = 5      # reference 2\npayment[54] = 3     # reference 55\ncount = 0\nfor ref in range(300):\n    if payment[ref] == 5:\n        count = count + 1\nprint(\"Students making five payments:\", count)</pre>",
