"model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Network interface card</b> — Location: inside Sharon's desktop computer (in an expansion slot or built into the motherboard), with an Ethernet port at the back. Function: connects the desktop to the network by cable and gives it a unique MAC address so it can send and receive data.</p><p><b>Wireless network adaptor</b> — Location: built into (or plugged by USB into) each laptop, tablet or phone. Function: lets the device send and receive data over Wi-Fi radio signals so it can join the network without a cable.</p><p><b>Modem</b> — Location: where the TelEm/ISP line or cable enters the house, connected between that line and the router. Function: converts (modulates/demodulates) the signals on the ISP's line into digital data the home network can use, and back again.</p><p><b>Router</b> — Location: at the centre of the home network, connected to the modem, with the desktop cabled to it and the wireless devices linked to it. Function: directs (routes) data packets between the devices on the home network and the Internet, assigns IP addresses and shares the one Internet connection (often also acting as the wireless access point).</p><p><b>(b)</b> 1. Check that the Ethernet cable is firmly plugged into both the desktop and the router, and that the network-port lights are on (try another cable or router port). 2. Restart the desktop (and router) or run the Windows network troubleshooter to check the network adapter is enabled and has a valid IP address (<code>ipconfig</code>).</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b> 1. Select the data A2:G7 (book types and Monday–Saturday sales, plus H if a total series is wanted). 2. Click the Insert tab. 3. Choose Column ▸ Clustered Column. 4. Add the chart title 'Book Sales for Week 2' and check the legend/axis labels; move the chart into place.</p><p><b>(ii)</b> A (clustered) bar chart: it shows the same comparisons but the bars run horizontally, with the categories on the vertical axis. (A stacked column chart would show one column per day divided into book types.)</p><p><b>(iii)</b> A pie chart — best used to show how parts make up a whole, e.g. each type of book's share of the 1,775 books sold in the week (one data series only). (A line graph is best for showing a trend over time.)</p><p><b>(iv)</b> <code>=SUM(B3:B7)</code> (result 350).</p>",
"key": "<p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) For EACH device: location (1), function (1) (8).</li><li>(b) 1 each for TWO suitable instructions (2).</li><li>(c)(i) 1 per step in order: select data; Insert; choose column chart; add title/labels (4). (ii) Chart named (1), difference (1). (iii) Chart named (1), best use (1). (iv) =SUM (1), B3:B7 (1); =B3+B4+B5+B6+B7 earns 1.</li></ul>",
