"model": "<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Payment</b> and <b>Interest</b> (Payment = MonthlyPayment − Interest, so Payment + Interest = $1,000).</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> Line 11. The new balance is stored in NewPrincipal, so Principal never changes. Correct it to: <code>Set Principal ← Principal − Payment</code>. (Good practice: also initialise TotalInterest, TotalPayment and Count to 0 before Line 5.)</p><p><b>(ii)</b> It shows up at the <b>execution (run/testing)</b> stage — a logic error is not detected by the translator. Consequence: Principal stays at 10,000, so the WHILE condition is always true; the loop never ends (infinite loop), printing the same line over and over and never printing the totals.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A WHILE (pre-test, condition-controlled) loop.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The number of payments is not known in advance — it depends on how the interest reduces the principal — so the loop must keep repeating until a condition (Principal &gt; 0) becomes false; the WHILE also checks the condition before each payment, so no payment is made when nothing is owed.</p><p><b>(e)</b> A REPEAT…UNTIL loop: the body (calculate interest, payment, print, update principal) is executed first and the condition <code>UNTIL Principal &lt;= 0</code> is tested at the end, so it always runs at least once — suitable because a new loan always needs at least one payment. (A FOR loop could be used only if the number of payments, 11, were calculated first.)</p><p><b>(f)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Principal</th><th>Payment</th><th>MonthlyInterest (interest charged)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Loop 1</td><td>$10 000</td><td><b>$900.00</b></td><td><b>$100.00</b></td></tr><tr><td>Loop 2</td><td><b>$9 100</b></td><td><b>$909.00</b></td><td><b>$91.00</b></td></tr><tr><td>Loop 3</td><td>$8 191</td><td><b>$918.09</b></td><td>$81.91</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Working: Loop 1: Interest = 10,000 × 0.01 = 100; Payment = 1,000 − 100 = 900; new principal 9,100. Loop 2: Interest = 91; Payment = 909; new principal 8,191. Loop 3: Interest = 81.91; Payment = 918.09. (The column headed MonthlyInterest in the original holds the interest charged each month.)</p><p>Corrected program (Pascal), tested: it makes 11 payments, total paid to principal $10,410.15, total interest $589.85 (the last payment overpays, which a Grade One answer would point out).</p><pre>program LoanSchedule;\nvar\n  Principal, MonthlyPayment, MonthlyInterest: real;\n  Interest, Payment, TotalInterest, TotalPayment: real;\n  Count: integer;\nbegin\n  Principal := 10000; MonthlyPayment := 1000; MonthlyInterest := 0.01;\n  TotalInterest := 0; TotalPayment := 0; Count := 0;\n  writeln('Principal':12, 'Payment':12, 'Interest':12);\n  while Principal &gt; 0 do\n  begin\n    Interest := Principal * MonthlyInterest;\n    Payment := MonthlyPayment - Interest;\n    TotalInterest := TotalInterest + Interest;\n    TotalPayment := TotalPayment + Payment;\n    writeln(Principal:12:2, Payment:12:2, Interest:12:2);\n    Principal := Principal - Payment;\n    Count := Count + 1;\n  end;\n  writeln(TotalPayment:0:2, ' ', TotalInterest:0:2, ' ', Count);\nend.</pre><p>Python:</p><pre>principal = 10000\nmonthly_payment = 1000\nmonthly_interest = 0.01\ntotal_interest = total_payment = 0\ncount = 0\nprint(f\"{'Principal':&gt;12}{'Payment':&gt;12}{'Interest':&gt;12}\")\nwhile principal &gt; 0:\n    interest = principal * monthly_interest\n    payment = monthly_payment - interest\n    total_interest += interest\n    total_payment += payment\n    print(f\"{principal:12.2f}{payment:12.2f}{interest:12.2f}\")\n    principal = principal - payment\n    count += 1\nprint(round(total_payment, 2), round(total_interest, 2), count)</pre><p><b>(g)(i)</b> Telecommuting means working from home (or anywhere away from the office) using a computer, the Internet and tools such as e-mail, Teams/Zoom and cloud files to stay connected to the workplace. It was impactful because businesses, schools and government offices could keep operating during COVID-19 lockdowns while staff stayed safe, and it saves travel time and costs.</p><p><b>(ii)</b> A denial-of-service (DoS) attack floods a server or network with so many requests (traffic) that it becomes overloaded and legitimate users cannot reach it. Example: attackers use a botnet of infected computers (a distributed DoS) to bombard an online banking website so customers cannot log in to pay bills.</p><p><b>(iii)</b> Similarity: both are wireless technologies that send data using radio waves. Difference: Bluetooth is short range (about 10 m) and connects two devices directly (pairing), whereas Wi-Fi has a longer range (about 50–100 m), higher speed and connects many devices to a network and the Internet through a router/access point.</p><p><b>(iv)</b> Advantages: the software is updated automatically by the provider; it can be accessed from any device with a browser, anywhere; no need for powerful local hardware or installation; files are saved in the cloud. Disadvantage: it needs a reliable Internet connection (no Internet means no access) and usually an ongoing subscription fee; data is held by a third party.</p>",
