"model": "<p><b>(a)</b> 1 Special-purpose. 2 Custom-written. 3 General-purpose. 4 Customized.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> The document is first printed by a computer with some data already on it (so a person can read it); the person adds data by hand, such as a payment amount or pencil marks, and returns it. It is then read straight back into the computer by a machine (OCR/OMR reader), so it has 'turned around' from a human-readable output into machine-readable input without retyping.</p><p><b>(ii)</b> A GEBE electricity bill: the bottom stub is printed with the account number and amount due; the customer writes the amount paid and returns it with payment; the stub is scanned by OCR so the payment is recorded automatically. (Or a CXC multiple-choice answer sheet pre-printed with the candidate number and read by OMR.)</p><p><b>(c)</b> Obtains the IP address — <b>2</b>; interprets and displays the HTML — <b>4</b>; sends an HTTP request — <b>3</b>; parses the URL to obtain the domain name — <b>1</b>.</p><p><b>(d)(i)</b> Heading 1 (Birdsong Pan Grove; Red Devils Football Academy) and Heading 2 (Pan Practice, Practice Schedules, Registration Form, Disclaimer).</p><p><b>(ii)</b> 1. Select (highlight) each main heading and click Home ▸ Styles ▸ <b>Heading 1</b>. 2. Select each sub-heading and click <b>Heading 2</b>.</p><p><b>(iii)</b> 1. Click at the very beginning of the document. 2. Insert a page break (Insert ▸ Blank Page/Page Break) so page 1 is empty, and click on that page. 3. Click the References tab ▸ Table of Contents. 4. Choose an automatic style (e.g. Automatic Table 1) — Word builds the table from the heading styles.</p><p><b>(iv)</b> Each entry in an automatic Table of Contents is a hyperlink to its heading, so hold <b>Ctrl</b> and click 'Registration Form' in the table; Word jumps straight to that heading on page 3.</p>",
"key": "<p><b>Mark scheme</b></p><ul><li>(a) 4 correct = 3 marks; 2–3 correct = 2; 1 correct = 1.</li><li>(b)(i) Human-readable output produced by computer (1); data added and read back by machine/OCR/OMR (1). (ii) Example application (1) and how used (1).</li><li>(c) All four correct = 3; two correct = 2; one = 1 (order 2, 4, 3, 1).</li><li>(d)(i) Heading 1 (1), Heading 2 (1). (ii) Select text (1); apply style from Home ▸ Styles (1). (iii) 1 mark per step in order (4). (iv) Ctrl (1) + click entry/hyperlink (1).</li></ul>",
