"model": "<p><b>(a)(i)</b> <code>=COUNTA(A12:A15)</code> — counts the four text entries (result 4).</p><p><b>(ii)</b> Currency format (e.g. $100.90); percentage format (e.g. 17%); number of decimal places (e.g. 2 d.p.) — also comma/thousands separator and accounting format.</p><p><b>(iii)</b> The dollar signs make <b>$B$4</b> an absolute reference: when the formula is copied down from D12 to D15, it always uses cell B4 (the 17% tax rate), while the relative reference <b>B12</b> changes to B13, B14 and B15 so each T-shirt's own price is used.</p><p><b>(iv)</b> In E15: <code>=(B15-C15)*(1+$B$4)</code>, where C15 holds the discount <code>=B15*B9</code> ($3.62). Result: (120.75 − 3.62) × 1.17 = <b>$137.04</b>. (Equivalent: <code>=B15-C15+D15</code> if D15 holds the tax on the discounted price, <code>=(B15-C15)*$B$4</code>.)</p><p><b>(v)</b> In <b>E17</b>: <code>=AVERAGE(E12:E15)</code> (≈ $91.63 when all four final costs are calculated the same way).</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> A (Wi-Fi) <b>hotspot</b> — a wireless access point.</p><p><b>(ii)</b> <b>Network adapter:</b> the hardware in each phone, tablet or laptop that lets the device connect to and send/receive data on the network. <b>Wi-Fi:</b> the wireless technology that carries the data between the devices and the access point using radio waves, so no cables are needed. <b>Router:</b> forwards (routes) data packets between the devices on the store network and the Internet, assigning each device an IP address and sharing the one connection. <b>Modem:</b> converts the signals on the ISP's line (e.g. from TelEm) into digital data for the router and back again (modulation/demodulation).</p><p><b>(c)(i)</b> <b>FALSE.</b> S MOD P = 5 MOD 2 = 1 (the remainder), and 1 = 2 is FALSE; T (3) is not equal to FALSE, so the whole statement is FALSE. (Read as T == (S MOD P): 3 == 1 is also FALSE.)</p><p><b>(ii)</b> <b>FALSE.</b> S == P is 5 == 2, which is FALSE; T &gt;= N is 3 &gt;= 10, which is FALSE; FALSE AND FALSE = FALSE (AND needs both to be TRUE).</p><p><b>(d)</b> A OR B: TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Arithmetical operators (+, −, *, /, MOD, DIV) are used to perform calculations, in process (rectangle) boxes and assignment statements, e.g. <code>Total ← Price * Qty</code>. Relational operators (&lt;, &gt;, =, &lt;&gt;, &lt;=, &gt;=) are used to compare values in conditions, in decision (diamond) boxes and IF/WHILE/UNTIL statements, e.g. <code>IF Age &gt;= 18 THEN</code>.</p>",
