"model": "<p><b>(a)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field Name</th><th>Data Type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Activity Number</td><td>Text</td></tr><tr><td>Activity Description</td><td>Text</td></tr><tr><td>Event</td><td>Date/Time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(b)</b> (i) Number (Integer). (ii) ACTIVITY: <b>Activity Number</b>. ALLOCATION: <b>Allocation Number</b>. (iii) <b>Activity Description</b> (each value is unique, so it could also identify a record). (iv) <b>Activity Number</b> (primary key in ACTIVITY, foreign key in ALLOCATION).</p><p><b>(c)</b> (i) <b>SumOf</b>Money Returned (a Sum/Total query). (ii) The total money returned by all parents for all the activities (1500 + 1500 + … + 200 = 13 500). (iii) ALLOCATION.</p><p><b>(d)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Activity Description</th><th>CountOfParent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bingo</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Dinner</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Karaoke</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Pageant</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>(e)</b> (i) The records are grouped by <b>Parent</b>. (ii) They are sorted by <b>Activity Description</b> (ascending within each parent — the parents themselves are not in alphabetical order). (iii) <b>Sum</b> of <b>Tickets Taken</b> (a subtotal for each parent and a Grand Total of 950). (iv) <b>Summary of Allocations</b>.</p><p>(f)</p><pre>SELECT SUM([Money Returned]) AS [SumOfMoney Returned]\nFROM ALLOCATION;\n\nSELECT ACTIVITY.[Activity Description], COUNT(ALLOCATION.Parent) AS CountOfParent\nFROM ACTIVITY INNER JOIN ALLOCATION\n     ON ACTIVITY.[Activity Number] = ALLOCATION.[Activity Number]\nGROUP BY ACTIVITY.[Activity Description];</pre><p>(g) Allocation 9 (Karaoke) is entered as 'G. Jones' in Table 2, but the report lists that Karaoke allocation under 'G. John' — so either the table or the report is wrong, and a query on 'G. John' would miss it. With a PARENT table (ParentID primary key, ParentName), ALLOCATION would store ParentID as a foreign key; each name is typed once, and referential integrity stops allocations to non-existent parents.</p>",
